"selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284063 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186282487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284063 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4e6e1327-d962-5a5a-86df-5a6da163058c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4b22f39-03e5-53f5-bf7c-13b93c3d9aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e67e934-1049-5a47-aaca-94c1d2a3bfc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284757 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284757 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177769" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284757 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284757 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284757 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eeb4fa8d-201a-5a9d-8ffa-ae311083bf29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284757 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284759 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284759 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284759 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284759 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)$, where $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284759 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a26587c8-f4e3-50c7-8549-c17141f2d144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284759 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x)\\cdot Q(y) & =x\\cdot y \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-7)+1\\cdot (-5)+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1045 "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186406657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406635 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "11d38e3f-6339-5429-8d89-8272fca9a35c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406636 #edb/ref 1080 "$U$ and $W$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406636 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb